FSEEK(3)                       P32W Unix                        FSEEK(3)

NAME
       fseek, ftell, rewind - reposition a stream

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       int fseek(FILE *f, long off, int whence);
       long ftell(FILE *f);
       void rewind(FILE *f);

DESCRIPTION
       fseek sets the position of the stream f for the next read or
       write: off bytes from the start of the file (whence SEEK_SET,
       0), from the current position (SEEK_CUR, 1) or from the end
       (SEEK_END, 2). It first writes out any buffered output, then
       discards the input buffer and any character pushed back by
       ungetc(3), clears the end-of-file flag, and calls lseek(2).
       For SEEK_CUR on a stream open for reading, off counts from
       the position the program has read to, not from where the
       descriptor is.

       ftell returns the stream's current position in bytes from the
       start of the file, counting buffered input and output.

       rewind is fseek(f, 0, SEEK_SET) followed by clearerr(3).

RETURN VALUE
       fseek returns 0, or -1 if lseek(2) failed (errno set: ESPIPE
       on a pipe or a terminal, EINVAL for a position before the
       start). ftell returns the position, or -1 with errno set.

EXAMPLES
       fseek(f, 0L, SEEK_END);     /* the size of a file */
       size = ftell(f);
       rewind(f);

SEE ALSO
       lseek(2), stdio(3), fopen(3)

BUGS
       A failure to write the buffered output is not reported by
       fseek (but ferror(3) shows it). ftell does not allow for a
       character pushed back by ungetc, and is wrong on a stream
       opened with + after reading (see fopen(3)). long is 32 bits,
       so positions stop at 2^31 - 1. There is no fgetpos or
       fsetpos.
